# Alexandre Vanderbuck Withers — claimant of Skull Keep

## S3E12 FINAL: Physically rescued from pyramid via Darmah first portal

[Delvers S3E12](../../episodes/S3E12.md) confirms **ALEXANDRE WITHERS ACTUALLY ALIVE/STABLE and carried through FIRST completed Teleportation Circle to Guild basement** along with Buck Wildfire, other enslaved workers and Phelan, aided by skeletal minotaurs/Edgar Boonfellow. **Not left behind in collapsing pyramid or declared dead**. Later Guild doctors tend rescued civilians. **NO additional voluntary confession, interrogation, judicial outcome or family reunion** shown; his earlier S2E12 suspicious hiring statement remains his allegation and is NOT disproved/proven by Khalazar defeat. [S3E12 dossier](../../episodes/S3E12.md).

## S3E11: Physically found at work inside black pyramid, no rescue

[Delvers S3E11](../../episodes/S3E11.md) reveals **ALEXANDRE VANDERBUCK WITHERS ACTUALLY PHYSICALLY PRESENT** in lower statue workshop of [Dawnless Dunes black pyramid](../places/dawnless-dunes-pyramid.md), not dead at prior S2E12 pit fall. Dominic scouts him and reports to Koh's psychic network; Koh later enters same room and directly sees Withers at work on the **~90ft unfinished Khalazar face statue scaffold**, receiving parts from dismantled spider and dark ichor plumbing. Buck Wildfire also there, both look dishevelled and **seem compulsively working**, no voluntary confirmation/clear memory. **No restoration of free will, escape, testimony or arrest in S3E11**, evacuation is only being prepared at cutoff. Retain his S2E12 admission as HIS OWN allegation, not proof he authored pyramid or controls slaves. [S3E11](../../episodes/S3E11.md).

## S2E12: highly incriminating statement and unknown fate

The wealthy fundraiser **ACTUALLY ATTENDS the Bronze Ball**, donor speech and auction, in **ill-fitting GOLD vest with black vinework, decorative shoulder pieces and a small tilted beret**, seated with **Mayor Malterby** and fellow patrons. **Anthela says he looks familiar from nightmares**—her impression, not proof of an actual visit. When a **smoky black-eyed possessing Khalazar voice through Selenar Everbloom** says Withers **FAILED TO DELIVER** and threatens to take all, **WITHERS RESPONDS IN THE PARTY'S HEARING: “I hired the Broscales. I opened the doors. There's nothing more I could do.”** Attribution is his *own statement under distress*, not independent verified contract: what “doors” he opened, whether same Broscales, what he promised, culpability and exact time remain unresolved. **Wildfire physically grabs him by collar** in auction room; **a fresh pit opens under BOTH and they FALL underground**. **No proven fatality, rescue or later confession**, and the **Hereditary Drone remains ACTIVE at second arc cutoff**. [Finale](../../episodes/S2E12.md), ~02:15–02:41.

## Introduction

Alexandre Vanderbuck Withers is a prosperous, talkative aristocrat who arrives in the Darkest Delvers' lives with wine on his breath and a determination to recover his family's ancestral holiday residence. His magnificent mustache, carefully chosen velvets and warm cheeks make him appear as much a jovial patron as a serious man of business. He travels in a comfortable coach and expects his employees to recognize the importance of preserving fine furniture while fighting undead.[^introduction]

## Family interests and anxiety

He calls the long-abandoned **Enzo Keep** his property and says he has repurchased it after some hundred and thirty years. A previous bronze group has gone missing, and Alexandre is impatient for results. He promises a generous premium for returning his antiques intact, yet repeatedly cautions his new agents not to stare into the keep's mirrors. These warnings could reflect reports he has heard, family lore or something more; his precise knowledge of the old magic remains unknown.

For all his talk of danger, Alexandre does not attempt the expedition himself. He and Aegis retire to a guest house beyond the keep, leaving the adventurers to negotiate its inhabitants and the flameskull.[^contract]

## The rooms he does not know

In Atomer's financial district, Withers's home is an imposing limestone building with fine columns and a collection of statuary. It was once a financial house, and he acknowledges that its many old rooms may still hold secrets beyond his acquaintance. A burrowing bulette tears the pavement open before its entrance and exposes a chamber below ground. Koh enters and finds a clean stone slab fitted with restraint straps, recent traces of visitors and a thick door into the estate. When asked, Withers politely disclaims knowledge of the room and offers to see to it. The Delvers leave unwilling to accuse him on evidence they cannot yet fully interpret.[^room]

The guildmaster Cassius quietly fears Withers's collection of costly jade and other artifacts may mask an illicit purpose. His earlier contract to clear Skull Keep and his financing of guild ventures now look less innocent to Cassius than they once did. That suspicion provokes a covert investigation involving former thieves, but nothing recovered in the chamber establishes who used it or why. Withers himself remains unaware, at least in played dialogue, of the clandestine assignment intended to enter his estate.[^plot]

## The night of the missing treasures

Withers's wealth invites the attention of both thieves and the guildmaster who suspects him. While he is reportedly away at a party, Koh and his companions break into his estate through the ruined cellar wall. The private jade museum loses twenty-three small sculptures, and the sealed vault loses gems and unusual objects. A servant catches sight of an intruder moving under a heap of cloaks and shouts an alarm. The burglars flee the mansion in a waiting cart before Withers reaches home. Whatever he knows of the hidden prison and its elementally guarded chambers is not answered by his absence.[^s2e04]

## Current status

Alexandre (called **Alexander** in S2E04) **owns and occupies Withers Estate, sponsors the future Bronze Ball, and returns toward his mansion as the thieves escape**. The **former locked cellar door has been blown open**, the four elemental guardians have been bypassed and **Koh steals 23 portable jade artworks** while further unappraised jewelry and items are removed from lower vault. **A servant does raise an alarm**, but **Withers has not personally confronted or apprehended the burglars**, nor is his reaction, legal action or financial loss actually appraised. His earlier **claim not to know his old underground rooms remains unproved or disproved**, and **Cassius's suspicions about Skull Keep/artifact misuse remain unverified**. No player-facing evidence proves Withers knowingly committed a crime; **no house fire is observed**.[^s2e04][^s2e03]

[^s2e04]: [Delvers S2E04](../../episodes/S2E04.md), ~00:00–00:15 (owner away at party), ~02:42–03:03 (jade theft, lower vault, servant witnesses “coat monster”, actual cart escape as Withers approaches). His words/thoughts about losses **not depicted**.
[^room]: [S2E03](../../episodes/S2E03.md), ~00:55–01:13. Owner's assertion is **NPC testimony**; wrappers and straps objectively seen, occupants and use unknown. `Alexander/Alexandre` transcription variation; existing title follows older written reference.
[^plot]: [S2E03](../../episodes/S2E03.md), ~01:48–02:09. Cassius's theories of Withers abusing Skull Keep/artifacts are **his beliefs**, not independent confirmation.
[^s2e03]: [S2E03](../../episodes/S2E03.md), ~01:05–01:15 (in-person conversation), ~01:58–02:20 (off-books plan). **No mansion entry, proven theft, or financial judgment** by cutoff.
[^introduction]: [Delvers S1E06](../../episodes/S1E06.md), ~00:25–00:30. Working spelling `Alexandre Vanderbuck Withers`, sometimes transcribed `Vanderbilt`; Cassius briefly calls him `Mickels` before correcting himself to **Withers**. Not two separate patrons.
[^contract]: [S1E06](../../episodes/S1E06.md), ~00:25–00:33. An earlier party is missing; 350-gold stated base fee and up to 500 bonus each for antiques. The warnings about mirrors are his testimony.
[^aftermath]: [S1E06](../../episodes/S1E06.md), ~03:00–03:03. About 400 bonus gold mentioned without unambiguous units at close; final estate settlement and genealogy unshown.
